    support it natively. But it forces [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, and ES modules are blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m origins, so
    opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking would stop working — you'd need a local web server from then on. Moderately
    annoying to reverse.
  - [1mDual-format (browser global [3mand[0;1m [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m)[0m — the same file works in the browser and from Node, so [38;5;153msrc/[39m could
    read config too. Costs a small format-detection wrapper at the top of an otherwise plain file.

  My recommendation is the classic global. It matches how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is already written, keeps the double-click workflow
  intact, and adding more config values later is just more keys on one object. The dual-format option only pays off if
  the Node entry point in [38;5;153msrc/[39m actually needs these values — and right now [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a standalone greeter with no
  connection to the login app, so I'd treat that as YAGNI unless you tell me otherwise.

